},
	annote = {Notes - JC
Category:


J. Sarosi combines Tondu and Lopez’s equation design and Kerscher et al.’s equation to form an equation that can account for the deviation from perfect cylindrical and short length approximation to create a BPA model. 


The author also presented the model in comparison with a physical setup to test the BPA. 


Context:


The paper relies on all the original assumptions to the McKibben muscle equation. Applying the weave angle to the BPA muscle manufactured by Festo is rather odd. The Festo BPA has the same working principle as the original McKibben muscle but the construction is slightly different. There’s no information if the design different is important.


Correctness:


The parameters for the model are derived from the test BPA setup. However, the model is then compared to the same set up to validate, which seems odd. The model should have been compared to muscle of a different length and size.


Contributions:


The author contributed to the effort of modeling BPA by combining model from different groups


Clarity:


The author presented theoretical and experimental comparison in a different paper but not mentioned in this paper, which makes this paper to be short. This section probably should have been included in the original paper where the initial model was derived. 


},
