@inproceedings{huntModelingLengthEffects2017,
	address = {Cleveland, OH},
	title = {Modeling length effects of braided pneumatic actuators},
	volume = {58172},
	isbn = {978-0-7918-5817-2},
	url = {https://asme.pinetec.com/detc2017/data/pdfs/trk-8/DETC2017-67458.pdf},
	doi = {10.1115/detc2017-67458},
	abstract = {Braided pneumatic actuators (BPAs) are attractive for use in
bio-robots because they offer many muscle-like properties,
especially when compared to most other commercially available
robotic actuators. Unfortunately, the same properties that make
these actuators similar to muscles make them more difficult to
control. One such actuator manufactured by Festo, the MXAM-
10-AA, is frequently utilized in robotics because of its
commercial availability, durability, and force capability.
Although models for BPAs exist, the properties that make this
actuator more durable also make its behavior less like other
braided pneumatic actuators, especially for shorter actuator
lengths. Length specific models that do exist for Festo fluidic
muscles have numerous parameters that can only be found
experimentally by taking hundreds to thousands of data points
and performing a lengthy optimization process to fit parameter
values for each actuator in the system. This lack of
generalizability makes it difficult to build a new robot and begin
testing new control systems without significant startup time and
cost. The key contribution of this work is the development of a
generalizable actuator model that accounts for the geometry and
limitations of the actuator at shorter lengths. This empirical
model relates internal pressure, strain, stretching or contracting
state, and applied force on the MXAM-10-AA actuator. The
model is scalable to different length actuators by measuring their
resting length at zero pressure and their minimum contraction
length at maximum air pressure, and can be used for feedforward
length control. The model is evaluated on a robot leg with three
joints and 6 actuators, each with different length. The developed
controller, using the actuator model, controls the joints to within
± 3 degrees of the desired position for different desired torques
only using internal actuator pressure feedback. We also
demonstrate control speed by cycling a joint over 40 degrees of
rotation at varying frequencies.},
	language = {en},
	booktitle = {Volume {5A}: 41st {Mechanisms} and {Robotics} {Conference}},
	publisher = {ASME},
	author = {Hunt, Alexander and Graber-Tilton, Alexander and Quinn, Roger},
	month = aug,
	year = {2017},
	pages = {V05AT08A008},
	annote = {
Category

 This paper derives a generalized model for calculating the pressure needed in a 10 mm Festa BPA to generate a specific force and strain at the end effector. The model requires knowledge of the strain and force at maximum pressure (620 kPa). The paper continues to evaluate this model's effectiveness on a robot in which two antagonistic muscles are controlling joint angle.


Context

 This paper


Correctness

 The assumptions appear to be valid. One of the areas that could improve the results of the paper is removal of the use of kinked muscles on the robot. In a few spots, the author describes that due to the kinking, pressure filling in the muscle doesn't result in a change in length. Using unkinked muscles could improve the results and strengthen the conclusions. 


Contributions

 The main contribution of this paper is the model that is described above. The hope is that given a 10 mm Festo BPA, a user only needs to measure two parameters in order to have a functional model that will provide them pressure needed in the BPA in order to achieve a desired strain and force. It also describes how the model can be used in conjuction with a robot and bang bang controller to control a compliant robotic joint.


Clarity

The paper is well written, however, it almost feels like two papers in one. The first being a description of how the model was generated. The second paper being an implementation of the model on a controller for a robot.


},
	annote = {Notes - JC
Category:


In this article, the authors presented a new technique to model a Festo BPA where only a few initial parameters would need to be collected before the model can be constructed.


The model were tested on a that also uses the BPA muscles


Context:


The aims at controlling position of a mechanical joint element by feeding it pressure values. The input to the control system is torque and joint angle. However, surprisingly the angle parameter is not part of the negative feedback. Since the angle is not part of a feedback element, the angle of the joint can be adjusted if there’s no angle input to the control system.


On the robot, the pressure modulating the joint angle is a bang-bang controller. However, the position of the joint can still follow a commanded position very well - minus some overshooting.


Correctness:


The model was validated with an in-house quadroped robot.


Contributions:


The model is a good starting point for controlling Festo BPA, which has different physical characteristic compared to the traditional McKibben muscle. Such characteristic makes it difficult to model Festo BPA via the traditional analytical approach.


The model also helps filling the gap of relatively short length of BPA.


Clarity:


The paper is easy to understand and the modeling technique is simple to follow and implement.


},
